  "summary": "Japanese pubs that serve drinks with small dishes, emphasizing social drinking and sharing.",
  "description": "Izakaya interiors typically feature wooden furnishings, paper lanterns, and displays of food and drink. This aesthetic evolved from sake shops during the Edo period, reflecting Japanese drinking culture where food and drinks are enjoyed communally in relaxed, convivial atmospheres.",
  "culturalContext": "The izakaya — Japan's stay-drink pub — is a total sensory design: noren-curtained entrances, lantern-glow (chōchin) signage, counter-and-booth wood grain, otōshi starters, menu wall scroll calligraphy, and tapas-scale plate logic (edamame, karaage, yakitori smoke). The genre (Golden Gai's six-seat bars, Tabelog rankings) exports globally; izakaya aesthetics — intimacy, steam, chalkboard specials — now design restaurants from Brooklyn to Bangkok.",
